xnu 0311: the control channel can replace a file and kill a process

The USB control channel could drive a boot -- taps, keys, screenshots, reads of
the ring -- but not change it: the only writer of a file under the mounted root
was glblob=1 at mount, so replacing the Settings probe meant a kernel build and
a 16 GiB userdata flash, and the questions a single boot can answer were fixed
before it started.

"wfile PATH LEN" takes the bytes as "RLW1" + little-endian u32 offset + data
datagrams and writes the file once the contiguous prefix covers LEN; "wstat"
reports how much has arrived, so a host whose datagram was dropped resends
instead of starting over blind. The write truncates the target, so nothing may
have it mapped: "kill PID" comes with it, which also makes "what does this
system do when this daemon is absent" answerable inside the same boot, with
launchd's own restart throttle in the ring.

"say TEXT" prints "MONDRIAN MARK: TEXT". A run of taps and variants then
carries its own labels and the host reads the lines between two marks
afterwards: one boot answers a matrix of questions instead of one question.

The writer is mondrian_root_write() in mondrian_ufs.c, which glblob uses too,
so "write a file into the mounted root" has one implementation.

